# NOETHERVIM: SNIPPETS

A snippet turns a short trigger into a block of text with holes in it, and
moves you between the holes. It is the difference between typing
`\begin{theorem}` ... `\end{theorem}` and typing three letters.

This is core. LuaSnip is always there; the LaTeX bundle adds a large
library on top of it.

Table of contents:

- [Expanding and jumping](*expand*)
- [The Tab question](*tab*)
- [Getting unstuck](*unstuck*)
- [Finding out what you have](*browse*)
- [Writing your own](*writing*)
- [Switching one off](*disable*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## EXPANDING AND JUMPING *expand*

Type a trigger and press `<Tab>`. The snippet expands and the cursor lands
on the first placeholder.

    `<Tab>`     expand, or jump to the next placeholder
    `<S-Tab>`   jump to the previous placeholder

Type over a placeholder and `<Tab>` again to move on. Tabbing past the last
one finishes the snippet.

Snippets also appear in the completion menu as you type, so you do not have
to remember a trigger exactly. `<C-Space>` opens the menu on demand,
`<C-n>` and `<C-p>` move through it, and `<C-y>` accepts.

## THE TAB QUESTION *tab*

Tab is contested: it could jump a snippet, accept a completion, or insert a
tab. Rather than have you write keymaps, NoetherVim exposes the whole
philosophy as one setting in `lua/user/config.lua`.

    `"snippet"`    the default. Tab is reserved for snippet jumps. Pick
                 from the menu with `<C-n>` and `<C-p>`, accept with
                 `<C-y>`. Nothing competes for Tab.
    `"supertab"`   Tab accepts the highlighted completion, selecting the
                 top one if none is. Falls back to a snippet jump, then
                 to a literal tab. IDE muscle memory.
    `"navigate"`   Tab cycles the menu without accepting; `<C-y>` or
                 `<CR>` commits. The classic nvim-cmp behavior.

Set `completion_style` to one of those three. See
[the completion styles](noethervim-completion-style).

Fill in the setting that changes what Tab does:

The config key for the Tab philosophy is ___

## GETTING UNSTUCK *unstuck*

Occasionally a buffer behaves as though a snippet is still active when you
thought you had left it: text highlights oddly, or a new expansion lands in
the wrong place. LuaSnip leaves marks behind, and these clear them.

    `<C-u>`        insert or select mode: stop every snippet in the buffer
    `<Leader>U`    the same, from normal mode
    `<Leader>u`    unlink only the snippet you are in
    `<Leader>j`    jump back into the snippet under the cursor

Stopping and unlinking are different. Unlinking drops one snippet and hands
the cursor to its neighbor, leaving the marks; **stopping** unlinks
everything and clears the marks. Reach for `<C-u>` when a buffer feels
haunted. Your text is never touched either way.

`<Leader>j` is the useful one in daily work. Finishing a snippet is
normally the end of it, but if you filled a placeholder in wrongly, put the
cursor back in the snippet's text, press `<Leader>j`, and `<Tab>` moves
between its placeholders again without expanding a second copy.

With no snippet active and no marks left behind, `<C-u>` is just Vim's own
"delete what you typed on this line", untouched.

Fill in the key that stops every snippet in the buffer, from insert mode:

Stop all snippets, from insert mode: ___

## FINDING OUT WHAT YOU HAVE *browse*

    `<Space>cs`   every snippet loaded, for every filetype you have opened
    `<Space>gs`   grep the snippet sources
    `<Space>es`   open a snippet file for this filetype

`<Space>cs` is the catalogue. It lists each snippet, where it came from,
and whether it is switched on. `<CR>` opens the definition, so it doubles
as a way to read how a shipped snippet is built before writing your own.

`:checkhealth noethervim` counts how many are loaded per filetype and warns
when the same trigger is defined twice, which otherwise shows up only as a
duplicated entry in the completion menu.

## WRITING YOUR OWN *writing*

Snippets live in `LuaSnip/<filetype>/` inside your config directory. A file
there returns a list of snippets, and LuaSnip loads it alongside everything
the plugins ship.

`<Space>es` is the way in. It asks which filetype, then which file, listing
yours and the ones plugins ship together. A filetype you have no snippets
for yet says so and offers to create the file, seeded with a skeleton.

    `~/.config/nvim   · preamble.lua`
    `NoetherVim-Tex   · commands.lua`

**Saving the file puts it to work immediately.** That holds for a brand new
file for a filetype you had nothing for, and it holds in every other Neovim
you have open at the time. Nothing needs restarting.

Two cautions. A file owned by a plugin opens read-only, because a snippet
saved there is lost on the next update. And do not empty a snippet file: a
Lua file returning nothing errors the next time LuaSnip loads that
filetype. Delete the file instead.

## SWITCHING ONE OFF *disable*

A snippet whose trigger collides with a word you actually type is worse
than no snippet. In `<Space>cs`:

    `<C-x>`   switch it off
    `<C-y>`   switch it back on
    `<A-o>`   show only what is switched off
    `<C-o>`   copy a config entry for it
    `<Tab>`   add to the selection, so one key acts on several

Off means it leaves the completion menu and stops expanding.

There are two places to remember the choice, and they answer different
questions. `<C-x>` writes to Neovim's state directory: this machine, not
copied with your config. `snippets_disabled` in `lua/user/config.lua`
travels with your config and means you never want it anywhere. Where they
disagree the machine wins for the session, and the health check names the
snippet and both ways to settle it.

Nothing writes your config file for you; `<C-o>` puts the entry on the
clipboard with its owner and file already filled in.

